issues.body:
### What happened?

`parseWwwAuthenticate()` returns `{}` for a valid header containing Basic followed by Bearer. Multiple WWW-Authenticate fields are combined this way by `Headers`. The advertised metadata URL and step-up scopes are lost, which can send OAuth discovery to the wrong fallback.

It also reads parameters across challenge boundaries: `Bearer realm="mcp", Basic realm="gateway", scope="basic-only"` incorrectly returns the Basic challenge's scope.

### Steps to reproduce

Save as `repro.mjs` in the repo root and run `node repro.mjs`:
```js
import { parseWwwAuthenticate } from './packages/mcp/src/oauth/discovery.ts';
const headers = new Headers();
headers.append('WWW-Authenticate', 'Basic realm="gateway"');
headers.append('WWW-Authenticate',
  'Bearer resource_metadata="https://mcp.example/custom-metadata", scope="tools:read"');
console.log(parseWwwAuthenticate(headers.get('www-authenticate')));
// Actual: {}. The Bearer challenge alone parses correctly.
```

### Expected behavior

Select the Bearer challenge and read only its parameters, independent of challenge order (RFC 9110 §11.6.1). I would like to fix this and add regression tests for combined headers and parameter isolation.

### Version

pi-mcp 1.4.0; Node 24.19.0, Linux. Reproduced offline without extensions or real credentials. The affected file matches current main (blob `1d348ca98ff8b48ce691615cb6479f9b62c9279a`).

### What happened?

The Google stream parser coalesces all consecutive text parts (and all thinking parts) into one block and keeps only the last `thoughtSignature` via `retainThoughtSignature`. Parts without a `text` field are skipped entirely. On replay, `convertMessages` emits one part per block.

So the next request does not carry the signatures the way the model returned them:

- A signature that arrived on its own trailing empty-text part is moved onto the preceding text part.
- When two parts each carry a signature, only the last one survives.
- A part with only `thoughtSignature` (no `text` key) is dropped, together with its signature.

Google's thought-signature docs say to send each signature back inside its original `Part`, not to merge a signed part with an unsigned one, and not to combine two signed parts. They also note that when streaming, the signature may arrive on a part with empty text. Text-part signatures are not validated, so this does not cause a 400. Per the docs, the cost is reasoning quality on later turns.

I have seen the first shape (unsigned text, then `{ text: "", thoughtSignature }`) from a current Gemini model in normal multi-turn tool use. The other two I have only reproduced offline.

### Steps to reproduce

Offline, against `@earendil-works/pi-ai@1.4.0`: a local server streams the chunks below to `api/google-generative-ai` `stream()`, then the resulting message goes through `api/google-shared` `convertMessages()`.

```
== trailing empty-text signature
received : [{"text":"Hello "},{"text":"world"},{"text":"","thoughtSignature":"QUFBQQ=="}]
replayed : [{"text":"Hello world","thoughtSignature":"QUFBQQ=="}]

== two signed text parts
received : [{"text":"First.","thoughtSignature":"QUFBQQ=="},{"text":" Second.","thoughtSignature":"QkJCQg=="}]
replayed : [{"text":"First. Second.","thoughtSignature":"QkJCQg=="}]

== signature-only part
received : [{"text":"Answer"},{"thoughtSignature":"Q0NDQw=="}]
replayed : [{"text":"Answer"}]
```

<details><summary>Repro script (no network; <code>npm i @earendil-works/pi-ai@1.4.0</code>, then <code>node repro.mjs</code>)</summary>

```js
import http from "node:http";
import { stream } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/api/google-generative-ai";
import { convertMessages } from "@earendil-works/pi-ai/api/google-shared";

const SIG_A = "QUFBQQ==", SIG_B = "QkJCQg==", SIG_C = "Q0NDQw==";
const scenarios = {
  // Real-world shape: text streamed unsigned, signature on a trailing empty-text part.
  trailingEmptyTextSignature: [
    [{ text: "Hello " }], [{ text: "world" }], [{ text: "", thoughtSignature: SIG_A }],
  ],
  // Two separately signed text parts in one response.
  twoSignedTextParts: [
    [{ text: "First.", thoughtSignature: SIG_A }], [{ text: " Second.", thoughtSignature: SIG_B }],
  ],
  // Signature-only part (no text field).
  signatureOnlyPart: [
    [{ text: "Answer" }], [{ thoughtSignature: SIG_C }],
  ],
};
let current;
const server = http.createServer((req, res) => {
  let body = ""; req.on("data", d => body += d); req.on("end", () => {
    res.writeHead(200, { "content-type": "text/event-stream" });
    const chunks = scenarios[current];
    chunks.forEach((parts, i) => {
      const c = { candidates: [{ content: { role: "model", parts }, ...(i === chunks.length - 1 && { finishReason: "STOP" }) }] };
      res.write(`data: ${JSON.stringify(c)}\r\n\r\n`);
    });
    res.end();
  });
});
await new Promise(r => server.listen(0, r));
const model = { id: "gemini-3-pro-preview", name: "g", api: "google-generative-ai", provider: "google",
  baseUrl: `http://127.0.0.1:${server.address().port}/v1beta`, reasoning: true, input: ["text"],
  cost: { input: 0, output: 0, cacheRead: 0, cacheWrite: 0 }, contextWindow: 1e6, maxTokens: 8192 };
for (const name of Object.keys(scenarios)) {
  current = name;
  const s = stream(model, { messages: [{ role: "user", content: "hi", timestamp: Date.now() }] }, { apiKey: "x" });
  const msg = await s.result();
  const replay = convertMessages(model, { messages: [{ role: "user", content: "hi", timestamp: 0 }, msg] });
  console.log(`\n== ${name}`);
  console.log("received :", JSON.stringify(scenarios[name].flat()));
  console.log("stored   :", JSON.stringify(msg.content), msg.stopReason, msg.errorMessage ?? "");
  console.log("replayed :", JSON.stringify(replay.at(-1).parts));
}
server.close();
```

</details>

### Expected behavior

The replayed parts keep the signature boundaries as received, for the same provider and model:

```
[{"text":"Hello world"},{"text":"","thoughtSignature":"QUFBQQ=="}]
[{"text":"First.","thoughtSignature":"QUFBQQ=="},{"text":" Second.","thoughtSignature":"QkJCQg=="}]
[{"text":"Answer"},{"thoughtSignature":"Q0NDQw=="}]
```

Unsigned deltas can still be coalesced. Only the parts that carry a signature need to stay separate. Displayed text does not need to change.

Related: #7356 / #7362 fixed the replay side for signed empty blocks. This is the stream-parser side.

### Version

1.4.0 (same code on main at f715a15c)

`SqliteStorage` reads `next_id` once in `open()` (`dist/storage/sqlite/storage.js:92–95`). `mintId()` then counts in memory (`:127–131`), and `commit()` only takes `Math.max` against its own counter (`:124`); it never re-reads the stored `next_id`. When two storages have the same file open, in one process or in two, both hand out the same IDs. The second writer's commit is rejected by `checkGlobalIds` (`:473` / `:479`, `ID n already belongs to …`), and that fails the whole Session.

The README states a single-owner rule for IndexedDB (one owner at a time, behind a Web Lock). For SQLite, nothing enforces or documents it.

**Version:** `@earendil-works/pi-durable` 1.4.0, Node 26.8.1, Linux.

**Repro** (`node repro.mjs <empty-dir>`, run where the package resolves):

```js
// pi-durable 1.4.0 + Node's SQLite storage: a second opener of one file mints the IDs the first one holds.
import { join } from "node:path";
import { createSession } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable";
import { BACKGROUND_CONTEXT as context } from "@earendil-works/chord/context";
import { openNodeSqliteStorage } from "@earendil-works/pi-durable/storage/sqlite/node";
const path = join(process.argv[2], "s.sqlite");
const first = createSession(await openNodeSqliteStorage(path));
const second = createSession(await openNodeSqliteStorage(path)); // or a second process: nothing refuses it
const create = (session) => session.commit(async (tx) => (await tx.createConversation({ ownership: { kind: "ownerless" } })).id, context);
console.log("second created conversation", await create(second));
try {
	console.log("first created conversation", await create(first));
} catch (error) {
	console.log("first's commit failed:", error.message);
}
const end = await Promise.race([first.closed, new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(() => resolve({ reason: "open" }), 200))]);
console.log("first session:", end.reason, end.error?.message ?? "");
```

**Output:**

```
second created conversation 2
first's commit failed: ID 2 already belongs to conversation
first session: failed ID 2 already belongs to conversation
```

**Proposals, in order of preference:**

1. Take an exclusive lock at open, and fail clearly when another owner holds the file.
2. Re-read `next_id` inside the commit transaction, so two openers never mint the same ID.
3. Document the single-owner rule for SQLite and JSONL, as the README does for IndexedDB.

### Summary

On Telegram, a plugin sees `message_received` for an edited message only when the edit carries a location. Text and caption edits reach no plugin hook at all, even though the Gateway accepts the update and advances its polling offset.

### Problem to solve

`edited_message` and `edited_channel_post` are handled by the Telegram drain: the update is authorized and recorded for reply-chain context, and the handler returns `{ kind: "recorded" }` without dispatching an agent turn. That product decision is reasonable and is tracked separately in #136674 (whether an edit should re-trigger the conversation). This issue is about a different consequence of it.

Inside that recorded path, the only plugin-visible emission is `emitTelegramLiveLocationMessageHook`, and its builder returns `null` when `extractTelegramLocation(msg)` finds no location (`extensions/telegram/src/location-message-hook.ts`). So:

- edit a live location → `message_received` fires with `providerUpdate.kind: "edited_message"`;
- edit the text of a message → no `message_received`, no `before_dispatch`, no hook of any kind.

For a plugin that keeps a durable record of what the user said, the second case is a silent hole: the Gateway has accepted the new content — it is in the drain's spool, it is used for later reply-chain context, and the polling offset moves past it — but the new content never reaches any extension surface. The plugin cannot observe it, cannot revise its record, and cannot even report that something was missed, because nothing was delivered to it. Speech-to-text corrections and typo fixes are ordinary user behavior, so this is not an edge case for chat-based use.

The inconsistency also reads as accidental rather than intended: the same recorded path already emits the documented observation hook, just gated on one field of the message.

Synthetic reproduction on 2026.9.4:

```js
// edit-observer plugin
module.exports = {
  id: "edit-observer",
  register(api) {
    api.on("message_received", (event) => {
      api.logger.info(
        `message_received kind=${event.providerUpdate?.kind} id=${event.providerUpdate?.id} body=${event.content}`,
      );
    });
  },
};
```

1. Send `hello` from Telegram → the plugin logs `message_received kind=message ...`.
2. Edit that message to `hello there` → gateway log shows the update received, spooled, and the offset queued; the plugin logs nothing.
3. Send a live location and move → the plugin logs `message_received kind=edited_message ...` for each edit.

### Proposed solution

In the same recorded-update path, after the existing authorization gate, emit `message_received` for an edit that carries text or a caption, using the fields the contract already has:

- `providerUpdate.kind` = `edited_message` / `edited_channel_post`, so a plugin can tell an edit from a first send;
- `providerUpdate.id` = the original Telegram `update_id`;
- `providerUpdate.editedTimestamp` and the event `timestamp` = the edit time;
- `messageId` = the original message id, so the revision attaches to the message it replaces.

No new hook, no new field, no agent turn, and location edits keep their current behavior byte for byte. Edits with no text, caption, or location stay ignored.

Two things this deliberately does **not** do, to keep the change reviewable:

- It does not re-trigger the conversation. That is #136674 and is a product decision.
- It does not make the plugin's work a precondition for acknowledging the update. The emission is fire-and-forget, exactly like the existing location emission, and the drain already queues its polling offset before inbound hooks run. Whether an extension can hold an inbound acknowledgement until it has persisted the update is a separate durability question; it is filed separately as #147130, and the docs change in the PR says plainly that this hook is observation and not a delivery guarantee.

### Alternatives considered

- **A dedicated `message_edited` hook.** Cleaner name, but it adds a hook to the catalog for information `message_received` already carries via `providerUpdate.kind`, and every existing observer would have to learn about it. Happy to switch if maintainers prefer a distinct hook.
- **Let the plugin poll `getUpdates` itself.** Not viable: a second consumer on the same bot token competes for the shared update queue.
- **Read the edit out of the reply-chain cache later.** It is not an event, so there is no point at which a plugin knows a revision happened, and the cache is not a durable record.

### Impact

**Affected users:** plugin authors and operators who need a faithful record of user messages — memory/ledger plugins, compliance and retention tooling, anything that later answers "what did the user actually say".

**Severity:** medium to high for those users, because the loss is silent and the data is user content.

**Frequency:** every text or caption edit.

**Consequence without it:** an edited message's new content exists in the Gateway and in no extension.

### Evidence/examples

Synthetic repro above; source gate at `extensions/telegram/src/location-message-hook.ts` (builder returns `null` without a location) reached from `recordEditedMessageForReplyChain` in `extensions/telegram/src/bot-handlers.inbound-pipeline.ts`. A draft PR with the narrow change, the docs note, and focused tests is linked below.

### Do you plan to open a PR for this?

Yes — a draft PR is attached to this issue.

# [Bug]: Heartbeat duplicate suppression ignores a changed delivery destination

## Bug type

Behavior bug. Beta release blocker: No.

## Summary

In a deterministic source-level dispatch reproduction, after a heartbeat reply is settled as delivered to destination A, an identical text intended for destination B in the same policy session is suppressed as a duplicate before outbound delivery. The suppression key contains text and time but no route identity.

## Version and environment

OpenClaw source commit `81a4c771eff6cd4591ae7f9b1745d1ced399d5e6` (package version `2026.9.4`), Node v24.19.0 on Linux. Offline source-function harness; no running Gateway, model, provider chain, credentials, network sends or external services. OS distribution is not material to this deterministic JavaScript reproduction.

## Steps to reproduce

1. Check out the pinned source commit and use Node 24.16+.
2. Save the inline script below as `dedupe-probe.mjs`, then run `node dedupe-probe.mjs /path/to/openclaw-source`.
3. The dispatch portion invokes the actual `createHeartbeatDispatch` and full `prepareHeartbeatDispatchReply` functions, first with a synthetic `room-a` route. It calls `settle('delivered')` and preserves the resulting session state.
4. First run a control with the same route within the 24-hour window: the identical text is correctly suppressed.
5. Invoke the same path with the same text and policy-session state one second after the first delivery, changing the destination to synthetic `room-b`. Observe no prepared outbound reply and a `skipped` / `duplicate` event.
6. Run a second control using the original route just beyond the 24-hour window: the same text prepares a reply and settles successfully. The storage stub applies only mutations returned by the real settlement callback; neither suppressed attempt writes state.

The script accepts an arbitrary checkout path; no host-specific locations or private data are needed.

## Expected behavior

Duplicate suppression should apply to a previously delivered payload for the same effective delivery route. A prior send to another recipient is not confirmation that the current recipient received this reply. The outbound path explicitly supplies current channel, recipient, account and thread to its separate messaging-tool dedupe operation, but the heartbeat persisted-text check does not use those fields.

## Actual behavior and source

- [Dispatch duplicate check](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/81a4c771eff6cd4591ae7f9b1745d1ced399d5e6/src/infra/heartbeat-dispatch.ts#L405) compares trimmed text and a 24-hour timestamp window.
- [Successful settlement](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/81a4c771eff6cd4591ae7f9b1745d1ced399d5e6/src/infra/heartbeat-dispatch.ts#L490) stores `lastHeartbeatText` and `lastHeartbeatSentAt`, without the route used for that send.
- Probe output: `Confirmed dispatch behavior: after settling a delivery to room A, the same text to room B is suppressed as duplicate. No transport sends were made.`
- Controls pass: same-route suppression inside the window; same-route resend after the window. The classifier stub produces a normal successful, non-media, unstructured text outcome and does not make any duplicate decision. Duplicate comparison, suppression, and successful-state updates come from unchanged source functions.

## Impact and evidence limits

The reproduced impact is a prepared alert suppressed on a changed recipient within the same policy session. These inputs do not require an invalid cross-session state sharing assumption: the [preparation owner](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/81a4c771eff6cd4591ae7f9b1745d1ced399d5e6/src/infra/heartbeat-runner-execution.ts#L529) loads policy state by session while resolving delivery separately. A heartbeat destination can change while that session remains the same; for example, its explicit recipient configuration can change between runs. The harness supplies the resulting prepared inputs directly; it does not exercise that configuration-change path.

This is not a report of an observed external data disclosure, a provider issue, or an end-to-end live Gateway test. Source functions are extracted with TypeScript stripping; model classification, storage and transport seams are stubbed. A native Vitest regression through the module's registered owner and route-change integration coverage should accompany a fix.

## Fix direction, not a proposed patch

Key successful-delivery dedupe by normalized route identity as well as content, preserving same-route dedupe and handling existing stored records explicitly. Do not overload the human-readable `lastHeartbeatText` field with serialized route data. Any material persistence change should follow the repository's storage review checkpoint.

## Related issue check

I searched existing heartbeat deduplication and routing reports. [#135205](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/135205) concerns prompt timestamp context, [#43216](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/43216) missing duplicate suppression, and [#66380](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/66380) inherited stale state. I did not establish an existing issue for this destination-blind persisted text/time check.

<details>
<summary>Standalone source-level reproduction with controls</summary>

```javascript
