## What Problem This Solves

An admitted input whose transcript anchor is unavailable can retry the same unusable anchor and advance to another provider, even though a provider change cannot repair transcript continuity.

## User Impact

Stop that input after one failed attempt instead of repeating the same storage failure. Preserve its original error instead of trying to append a terminal assistant error through the invalid path. Genuine transient provider recovery remains unchanged. Input-specific failure-notice policy is intentionally a separate change; this PR does not claim to suppress every stale banner.

## Why This Change Was Made

Both missing-anchor emitters now produce a typed continuity failure. The existing native read-fence and writer-replacement errors, and exact serialized signatures, share one classifier. The SDK keeps these failures local; prompt/assistant handling and model fallback stop before unchanged-anchor response repair, account-health changes or another provider attempt. Existing cause/aggregate/suppressed error identities are retained.

The accepted-input owner's existing single admission refresh, original writer/session/entry/generation checks and side-effect replay barriers are unchanged. No recovery loop, replacement authority, schema, configuration or state store is added. #163655 already fixes the ordinary producer race; this is its remaining defensive failure-policy boundary, not another producer rewrite. #154904's dirty-index degradation policy and #182121's Workshop diagnostics are separate work and are not copied here.

